nearest(key, q, k[, #{metric: ".."}]) keeps the k incoming elements whose vector property key is closest to q, best first (the highest cosine or dot, the smallest l2 or hamming). It is an exact search: every incoming element is scored, with a bounded heap of k (O(n log k), never a full sort). Elements without the property, or whose value is not a vector, are skipped. Equal scores keep the input order, the earlier element first: the answer is deterministic, the same in a live graph, a view at a commit and a fork. q is a vector, a list of numbers or a traversal. A traversal runs for every scored element and must give the same vector each time (one query per search, "more like this" below); a query that depends on the element fails and names similarity(), which scores each element against its own vector. The metric follows the same rule as for similarity(). Without a metric argument, elements whose labels declare different metrics fail: one search ranks with one metric. Bulk counts as in limit(): a traverser that stands for n equal ones is n results, cut at k. .profile() shows the step as written, nearest("emb", vector([..]), 10, #{metric: "l2"}); the deadline (evaluationTimeout) and a cancel stop it while it scores. g.add_v("doc").property("title", "Graph databases 101").property("emb", vector([0.9, 0.1, 0.0])).iterate();
g.add_v("doc").property("title", "Vector search").property("emb", vector([0.1, 0.9, 0.1])).iterate();
g.add_v("doc").property("title", "Indexes").property("emb", vector([0.6, 0.6, 0.2], "i8")).iterate();
g.add_v("doc").property("title", "Draft").iterate();
g.v().has_label("doc").nearest("emb", vector([1.0, 0.2, 0.0]), 2).values("title").to_list(); // ["Graph databases 101", "Indexes"]
g.v().has_label("doc").nearest("emb", [0.0, 1.0, 0.0], 1, #{metric: "l2"}).values("title").next() // "Vector search" nearest() is the same as ordering by the score and keeping k, which is how to get the scores along with the elements: g.add_v("doc").property("title", "a").property("emb", vector([1.0, 0.0])).iterate();
g.add_v("doc").property("title", "b").property("emb", vector([0.6, 0.8])).iterate();
let q = vector([1.0, 0.1]);
g.v().has_label("doc").nearest("emb", q, 2) .project("title", "score").by("title").by(__.similarity("emb", q)) .to_list()

